      "narration": "Health policies define two perils. Accidental injury is bodily harm caused by an unexpected external event, such as a fall on the ice. Sickness is an illness or disease that first shows itself while the policy is in force, such as the strep throat Marcus’s daughter develops. The distinction has consequences. An accident-only policy pays nothing for sickness. Many policies apply a probationary period to sickness but not to accidents, so an illness in the first weeks may be excluded while an accident is covered. Accidental death and dismemberment benefits respond only to accidents.",
